WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.568 --> 00:00:06.305
No matter who you ask
or what year you're
talking about,

00:00:06.338 --> 00:00:10.777
the idea of flying cars
always seems to come up

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:15.048
as cementing
when the future has arrived.

00:00:16.715 --> 00:00:18.651
<i> So, what future
 were we promised,</i>

00:00:18.684 --> 00:00:20.319
<i> how far have we gotten,</i>

00:00:20.352 --> 00:00:23.189
and how much further up
do we have to go?

00:00:25.357 --> 00:00:28.094
<i> And I'm Marques Brownlee
 and I review dope new tech.</i>

00:00:28.127 --> 00:00:30.663
<i> But on this show,
 I'm rewinding the clock</i>

00:00:30.696 --> 00:00:32.298
<i> to look at the tech
 of the past</i>

00:00:32.331 --> 00:00:35.768
<i> that we thought
 would be our future.</i>

00:00:35.801 --> 00:00:39.372
<i> This is "Retro Tech:
 Flying Cars."</i>

00:00:39.405 --> 00:00:41.641
Hey, what's up? MKBHD here.

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:43.342
I know I'm unboxing
something today

00:00:43.375 --> 00:00:45.211
that has something
to do with flying cars,

00:00:45.244 --> 00:00:47.447
that it's this, uh,
iconic piece of tech

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.749
that apparently
was going to usher us

00:00:49.782 --> 00:00:52.752
into this new era
of personal transportation.

00:00:52.785 --> 00:00:54.587
We'll see about that.

00:00:56.155 --> 00:00:57.690
This is definitely
the biggest box

00:00:57.723 --> 00:00:59.592
we've had on this show.

00:00:59.625 --> 00:01:02.395
There is no indication though
anywhere on this box what it is.

00:01:04.163 --> 00:01:06.833
It's like a riddle.
What would be a piece of tech

00:01:06.866 --> 00:01:10.303
that would promise
to revolutionize
personal transport,

00:01:10.336 --> 00:01:13.206
but also not fit
in a smaller box than this?

00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:15.775
I'm guessing it's not
a flying car.

00:01:15.808 --> 00:01:21.547
There is a giant wheel.
Oh, is this the Segway?

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:25.384
Oh, it's a Segway.
I know what a Segway is. Okay.

00:01:25.417 --> 00:01:28.721
I cannot wait
to try to ride this.

00:01:32.158 --> 00:01:34.393
It's really hea--
oh, well, it has to be heavy

00:01:34.426 --> 00:01:36.763
because it's got
to counterbalance.

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:42.869
There it is,
the original Segway.

00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:45.271
Is it a flying car? No.

00:01:45.304 --> 00:01:49.142
Is it even a hovercraft? No.
Well, let's get into it.

00:01:49.175 --> 00:01:52.411
This is the
"Welcome to the evolution

00:01:52.444 --> 00:01:55.448
of personal mobility" pamphlet.

00:01:55.481 --> 00:01:57.416
Lots of warnings.

00:01:57.449 --> 00:01:59.519
"Don't roll this over bumps.

00:01:59.552 --> 00:02:01.587
Don't ride this
over curbs and steps

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.189
and obstacles
and steep slopes

00:02:03.222 --> 00:02:05.391
and slippery surfaces
and loose materials."

00:02:05.424 --> 00:02:06.559
Whew.

00:02:06.592 --> 00:02:10.363
All right.
Can I get a helmet?

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:13.866
Okay, I'm going
to be the expert.

00:02:13.899 --> 00:02:16.402
I'm going to put all the swag
that came with it on.

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:19.672
This is a Segway armband.

00:02:19.705 --> 00:02:21.941
The expert would probably
have this on, right?

00:02:21.974 --> 00:02:24.510
It's too bad
I'm too swole for this.

00:02:24.543 --> 00:02:26.579
I'll probably have
to put it on my wrist.

00:02:26.612 --> 00:02:29.282
That is nerdy. Wow.

00:02:29.315 --> 00:02:30.783
I hate to break it
to you, Segway,

00:02:30.816 --> 00:02:33.219
but this is the nerdiest part
of this whole thing.

00:02:33.252 --> 00:02:35.355
There you go.
Let's fire it up.

00:02:36.989 --> 00:02:38.191
Oh!

00:02:40.559 --> 00:02:42.562
Okay.

00:02:50.736 --> 00:02:53.273
I'm a little dizzy.
I'll be right back.

00:02:54.873 --> 00:02:56.576
Wow.

00:02:59.845 --> 00:03:01.647
That was fun.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:04.650
Is this a flying car? No.

00:03:04.683 --> 00:03:06.853
But this is my segue
into the next segment.

00:03:13.859 --> 00:03:17.463 line:0%
I'm gonna have you
check underneath your chair.

00:03:17.496 --> 00:03:20.866
( laughs )

00:03:20.899 --> 00:03:23.002 line:0%
Oh, yeah, it's my buddy.!

00:03:23.035 --> 00:03:26.439 line:0%
Oh, guys. It's the '80s?

00:03:26.472 --> 00:03:27.940 line:0%
It's the '80s in a car?

00:03:27.973 --> 00:03:30.543 line:0%
A DeLorean, a DMC-12,
with Mr. Fusion on the back.

00:03:30.576 --> 00:03:33.012 line:0%
This is the flying car
that most people think of

00:03:33.045 --> 00:03:34.714
when they think of
a flying car.

00:03:34.747 --> 00:03:36.515
Marques: All right,
I guess let's walk back

00:03:36.548 --> 00:03:38.985
to, like, the concept
of flying cars in general.

00:03:39.018 --> 00:03:43.623
Why was this such
an obsession for us?

00:03:43.656 --> 00:03:45.691
Negin:
<i> It's kind of not surprising
 that Americans</i>

00:03:45.724 --> 00:03:47.360
would be obsessed
with flying cars

00:03:47.393 --> 00:03:48.995
because we're just
a car culture.

00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:51.764
- ( engine revs )
- ( tires squeal )

00:03:51.797 --> 00:03:53.699
Tim:
<i> The key to the automotive
 interest in the United States</i>

00:03:53.732 --> 00:03:56.035
has been always
that notion of freedom.

00:03:56.068 --> 00:03:57.870
Announcer:
<i> Well, the automobile</i>

00:03:57.903 --> 00:03:59.672
<i> has brought with it
 a new way of life.</i>

00:03:59.705 --> 00:04:02.675
And that freedom is just key
to the American experience.

00:04:02.708 --> 00:04:04.610
Cars are everything
in America.

00:04:04.643 --> 00:04:06.812
Even though we don't have
flying cars today,

00:04:06.845 --> 00:04:08.614
it's not because
we didn't try.

00:04:08.647 --> 00:04:10.950
Announcer:
<i> Just an ordinary plane,
 but, wait.</i>

00:04:10.983 --> 00:04:14.287
Negin:
<i> There's been multiple versions</i>

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:17.390
<i> of attempts of cars
 that were supposed to fly,</i>

00:04:17.423 --> 00:04:20.760
and they crashed.

00:04:20.793 --> 00:04:23.029
Tim:<i> Well, one of
 the earliest flying cars</i>

00:04:23.062 --> 00:04:24.764
<i> that actually looked like
 it had a road to production,</i>

00:04:24.797 --> 00:04:27.500
it was called the Aerocar,
way back in the 1940s.

00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:30.002
The Aerocar,
it had the wings in the back

00:04:30.035 --> 00:04:33.005
<i> that could turn it
 into a functioning airplane.</i>

00:04:33.038 --> 00:04:36.575
And this 60-mile-an-hour
car takes off.

00:04:36.608 --> 00:04:38.044
Announcer:
<i> Let's put traffic woes
 behind us</i>

00:04:38.077 --> 00:04:40.746
<i> and fly
 that wild blue yonder.</i>

00:04:40.779 --> 00:04:43.582
Joseph:
Is it a car that can fly,

00:04:43.615 --> 00:04:46.085
or is it just a plane
that can be broken down?

00:04:46.118 --> 00:04:48.054
( imitates explosion )

00:04:48.087 --> 00:04:49.522
Cooler heads prevailed,

00:04:49.555 --> 00:04:51.924
and the Aerocar
didn't move forward.

00:04:51.957 --> 00:04:53.859
Marques:
Okay, so, there were
actually some attempts

00:04:53.892 --> 00:04:55.761
at flying cars for real.

00:04:55.794 --> 00:04:57.096
But how did science fiction

00:04:57.129 --> 00:04:59.532
impact how we thought
about flying cars?

00:04:59.565 --> 00:05:02.702 line:91%
Science fiction has really
been obsessed with flying cars--

00:05:02.735 --> 00:05:05.438 line:0%
<i> movies like "Blade Runner"
 and "Star Wars,"</i>

00:05:05.471 --> 00:05:07.540 line:0%
<i> and of course
 "Back to the Future."</i>

00:05:07.573 --> 00:05:12.044 line:0%
The first time I ever
saw a flying car was
in "The Jetsons."

00:05:12.077 --> 00:05:15.614 line:0%
 Joseph:<i> The Jetsons
 have moving sidewalks</i>

00:05:15.647 --> 00:05:17.416 line:0%
<i> very similar to the ones
 that we see in airports,</i>

00:05:17.449 --> 00:05:19.051 line:0%
<i> television screens
 where they can video chat</i>

00:05:19.084 --> 00:05:20.686 line:0%
<i> like we do with FaceTime,</i>

00:05:20.719 --> 00:05:22.788 line:0%
<i> and they have flying cars.</i>

00:05:22.821 --> 00:05:24.857
So you say to yourself,
"Well, why not flying cars?

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:26.726
Why can't I have a flying car
in my garage?"

00:05:26.759 --> 00:05:28.894
Caseen:
<i> It's the ultimate fantasy
 for everyone.</i>

00:05:28.927 --> 00:05:30.596
But obviously there are
all sorts of challenges

00:05:30.629 --> 00:05:33.766
to making that happen
in real life.

00:05:33.799 --> 00:05:36.068
Marques:
<i>So, science fiction can help us
 imagine the future,</i>

00:05:36.101 --> 00:05:37.937
<i> but just envisioning
 a piece of technology</i>

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:39.739
<i> isn't enough
 to bring it into being.</i>

00:05:39.772 --> 00:05:41.474
<i> No matter
 how compelling the idea</i>

00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:43.075
<i> of a flying car may seem,</i>

00:05:43.108 --> 00:05:44.744
<i> the technology
 and the physics</i>

00:05:44.777 --> 00:05:46.612
<i> just doesn't seem
 to want to play along.</i>

00:05:46.645 --> 00:05:48.814
<i> So, today I'm meeting up
with fellow car tech enthusiast</i>

00:05:48.847 --> 00:05:50.649
<i> Gali Russell
 from HyperChange</i>

00:05:50.682 --> 00:05:53.819 line:0%
<i> to find out just how close--
 er, how far we are</i>

00:05:53.852 --> 00:05:57.056 line:0%
<i> from this future
 of flying cars.</i>

00:05:57.089 --> 00:05:59.125
Marques:
All right, so I guess
the basic challenge

00:05:59.158 --> 00:06:02.495 align:start position:0%
for a car like this today
to get it to fly

00:06:02.528 --> 00:06:04.797 align:start position:0%
is to get all the air
underneath it.

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:06.999 align:start size:91% position:9%
Yeah, it's a first principles
physics problem

00:06:07.032 --> 00:06:11.036
of literally moving
all of this air super quickly

00:06:11.069 --> 00:06:13.572
<i> with enough force
 to get some sort of lift-off.</i>

00:06:13.605 --> 00:06:15.107
Marques:
<i> Getting all that air
 underneath the car,</i>

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:16.442 align:start position:0%
that sounds like
a lot of energy.

00:06:16.475 --> 00:06:17.743 align:start size:81% position:19%
To lift it off the ground,

00:06:17.776 --> 00:06:20.846
we would need
1.6 megawatts of power.

00:06:20.879 --> 00:06:22.415 align:start position:0%
And that just gets you
into the air,

00:06:22.448 --> 00:06:23.416 align:start position:0%
and then you have
to stay there.

00:06:23.449 --> 00:06:24.850
Yeah.

00:06:24.883 --> 00:06:27.019 align:start position:0%
Even if we theoretically
solve that problem,

00:06:27.052 --> 00:06:30.523 align:start position:0%
how are we transferring
the air below the car?

00:06:30.556 --> 00:06:32.525 align:start size:75% position:25%
So, there's two schools
of thought

00:06:32.558 --> 00:06:34.860 align:start size:75% position:25%
of how we can actually
get enough force at once
to lift this thing.

00:06:34.893 --> 00:06:36.829
<i> You could have some sort
 of propeller system,</i>

00:06:36.862 --> 00:06:38.731 align:start position:0%
-<i> basically like a huge drone. </i>
-<i> Right.</i>

00:06:38.764 --> 00:06:40.499 align:start size:78% position:22%
And then you have
Elon and Tesla working on

00:06:40.532 --> 00:06:42.635 align:start size:75% position:25%
literally repurposing
rocket tech

00:06:42.668 --> 00:06:47.139
<i> with all these kind of
 mini cold gas thrusters
 to get it to lift off.</i>

00:06:47.172 --> 00:06:49.041
Okay, so, a system like this,

00:06:49.074 --> 00:06:51.043 align:start position:0%
if you were to build it
into this car,

00:06:51.076 --> 00:06:52.412 align:start position:0%
how would it work?
Where would you put it?

00:06:54.146 --> 00:06:56.215 align:start size:63% position:38%
So, this is where
the system would go.

00:06:56.248 --> 00:06:58.117 align:start position:0%
Okay, so the back seats
are just gone?

00:06:58.150 --> 00:06:59.785 align:start size:63% position:38%
Yeah, we're pushing
the laws of physics.

00:06:59.818 --> 00:07:01.821
You'd need to use
all the space.

00:07:01.854 --> 00:07:04.056
Marques:
<i> So, that's the cold
 compressed air,</i>

00:07:04.089 --> 00:07:06.725
<i> the pumps, the thruster,
 everything's going back here.</i>

00:07:06.758 --> 00:07:09.094 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Gali:<i> Yeah. </i>
-<i> That's a dramatic
 amount of space</i>

00:07:09.127 --> 00:07:12.064 align:start size:84% position:16%
- to remove from
  the inside of a car.
- Yeah.

00:07:12.097 --> 00:07:14.834
Marques:<i> So, basically,
 to get this nearly 5,000-pound
 car off the ground,</i>

00:07:14.867 --> 00:07:18.070
<i> you would need around
 1.6 megawatts of power</i>

00:07:18.103 --> 00:07:20.739
<i> for the car to hover
 with no forward movement.</i>

00:07:20.772 --> 00:07:22.575
<i> And this model's
 100-kilowatt hour battery</i>

00:07:22.608 --> 00:07:24.577
<i> would be able to sustain
 that hovering</i>

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:26.479
<i> for about 3 to 4 minutes,</i>

00:07:26.512 --> 00:07:28.147
<i> as opposed to
 the nearly 400 miles</i>

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:30.549
<i> you'd be able to travel
 if you stayed on the ground.</i>

00:07:30.582 --> 00:07:32.751
<i> And the calculations
 only account for the weight
 of the driver,</i>

00:07:32.784 --> 00:07:34.220
<i> so if you want to bring
 your friends along,</i>

00:07:34.253 --> 00:07:37.623
<i> your time in the air
 would be even shorter.</i>

00:07:37.656 --> 00:07:40.593
All right, so, we're making
our way into the future.

00:07:40.626 --> 00:07:41.894 align:start position:0%
The original idea
for flying cars though

00:07:41.927 --> 00:07:44.597 align:start position:0%
was just, like,
get from point A to point B.

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:47.233 align:start position:0%
Maybe there's better
versions of that future
that aren't flying.

00:07:47.266 --> 00:07:48.868 align:start size:66% position:34%
The Tesla in some way
embodies that

00:07:48.901 --> 00:07:50.603
<i> because what makes it
 so advanced</i>

00:07:50.636 --> 00:07:52.538
<i> is not that it can fly,
 but it has software.</i>

00:07:52.571 --> 00:07:53.873 align:start size:66% position:34%
You know, it can
kind of drive itself.

00:07:56.708 --> 00:07:59.111 size:88% position:44%
I wasn't around in the '60s,

00:07:59.144 --> 00:08:03.616 align:start position:0%
'70s, '80s, '90s when
we were imagining flying cars,

00:08:03.649 --> 00:08:06.619 line:0%
so when I first
was growing up and thinking
about the future of cars,

00:08:06.652 --> 00:08:09.588 line:0%
<i> I didn't think
 they should fly.</i>

00:08:09.621 --> 00:08:10.789 align:start position:0%
I thought, "Wow,
they should be more like

00:08:10.822 --> 00:08:12.291 align:start position:0%
the high-tech stuff
we have today."

00:08:12.324 --> 00:08:15.127
And now we're actually kind of
seeing that start to happen.

00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:16.896
<i> Our desire to innovate
 and create</i>

00:08:16.929 --> 00:08:19.965
<i> the next revolutionary
 technology is never-ending.</i>

00:08:19.998 --> 00:08:23.636
<i> And so while we may
 still be pretty far away
 from a world of flying cars,</i>

00:08:23.669 --> 00:08:26.272 line:0%
<i> there is another type
 of cutting-edge flying tech</i>

00:08:26.305 --> 00:08:29.008 line:0%
<i> that we've been dreaming
 about for decades.</i>

00:08:29.041 --> 00:08:30.743 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
- Hey!
- I need to borrow your...

00:08:30.776 --> 00:08:32.845 line:0%
Hover Board?

00:08:32.878 --> 00:08:37.249 line:0%
Nothing captured the imagination
more than Hover Boards.

00:08:37.282 --> 00:08:40.152
I wanted one.
I know as a kid, that's
something you could feel

00:08:40.185 --> 00:08:41.287
<i> you could talk your parents
 into buying you.</i>

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:43.188
A DeLorean? Not so much.

00:08:43.221 --> 00:08:44.823
I mean, what's better
than a skateboard

00:08:44.856 --> 00:08:46.292
than a skateboard that can
lift you off the ground?

00:08:46.325 --> 00:08:48.127
And I remember as a kid

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:51.931
believing that these things
might actually be real.

00:08:51.964 --> 00:08:55.000 line:0%
As a joke, Robert Zemeckis,
before the film came out,

00:08:55.033 --> 00:08:58.304 line:0%
gave an interview and said
that Hover Boards were real.

00:08:58.337 --> 00:09:00.005 line:0%
Zemeckis:
<i> They've been around
 for years,</i>

00:09:00.038 --> 00:09:02.575 line:0%
<i> it's just that parents' groups
 have not let</i>

00:09:02.608 --> 00:09:05.978 line:0%
the toy manufacturers make 'em,
and we got our hands on some.

00:09:06.011 --> 00:09:08.113
Tim:<i> Of course,
 it was all made up. It was all
 part of the marketing buzz.</i>

00:09:08.146 --> 00:09:10.316
But they made
so many kids excited
that everybody wanted 'em.

00:09:10.349 --> 00:09:12.685
But it never really came
to any sort of reality

00:09:12.718 --> 00:09:16.322
until we started to hear
about this revolution
in personal transport.

00:09:16.355 --> 00:09:19.725
So, it's the early 2000s
and we start to hear this buzz

00:09:19.758 --> 00:09:22.027 line:0%
about this transportation
product from Dean Kamen,

00:09:22.060 --> 00:09:24.263 line:0%
<i> who was already
 a well-respected inventor.</i>

00:09:24.296 --> 00:09:26.131 line:0%
It was codenamed "Ginger."

00:09:26.164 --> 00:09:28.000 line:0%
Tim:<i> Steve Jobs
 comes out and says,</i>

00:09:28.033 --> 00:09:30.069 line:0%
"This next thing is gonna be
bigger than the PC."

00:09:30.102 --> 00:09:33.038
It is rumored to be
the technological advance

00:09:33.071 --> 00:09:35.841
of this century,
an invention that could change

00:09:35.874 --> 00:09:37.142
the way people live.

00:09:37.175 --> 00:09:39.078
Bigger, perhaps,
than the Internet.

00:09:39.111 --> 00:09:42.247
It's finally time
for the big reveal.

00:09:42.280 --> 00:09:44.116 line:0%
Tim:<i> So, we all tune in
 to "Good Morning America,"</i>

00:09:44.149 --> 00:09:46.018 line:0%
<i> which was the biggest
 morning show of all time.</i>

00:09:46.051 --> 00:09:47.653 line:0%
Dean Kamen's
standing there talking

00:09:47.686 --> 00:09:49.188 line:0%
<i> with Charlie Gibson
 and Diane Sawyer.</i>

00:09:49.221 --> 00:09:50.990 line:0%
Negin:
<i> They lift the curtain...</i>

00:09:51.023 --> 00:09:52.091 align:start position:0% line:0%
Do we have to do
something here?

00:09:52.124 --> 00:09:56.862 line:0%
...and it is the Segway.

00:09:56.895 --> 00:09:58.297
It's the Segway.

00:09:58.330 --> 00:10:01.233 align:start position:0% line:0%
- Gibson: Okay.
- Tim:<i> It's basically a scooter.</i>

00:10:01.266 --> 00:10:03.702 line:0%
<i> The best part of that launch
 is Diane Sawyer</i>

00:10:03.735 --> 00:10:04.970 line:0%
